WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:08.674 --> 00:00:09.590
JENNY BROWN: Hi there.

00:00:09.590 --> 00:00:10.230
I'm Jenny.

00:00:10.230 --> 00:00:11.960
I'm a sales trainer
with Google Cloud.

00:00:11.960 --> 00:00:13.760
HEATHER CROSS: And hello,
again, from me, Heather.

00:00:13.760 --> 00:00:16.093
On today's episode, we'll
give you a high level overview

00:00:16.093 --> 00:00:16.864
of App Engine.

00:00:16.864 --> 00:00:18.780
JENNY BROWN: This is
really critical for folks

00:00:18.780 --> 00:00:20.160
that have a great
idea for an app,

00:00:20.160 --> 00:00:22.701
but don't want to mess around
with figuring out a development

00:00:22.701 --> 00:00:23.320
platform.

00:00:23.320 --> 00:00:24.750
HEATHER CROSS: That's right--
it gives them the autonomy

00:00:24.750 --> 00:00:27.084
to focus on writing their app
and perfecting their code.

00:00:27.084 --> 00:00:29.000
JENNY BROWN: Make sure
to stick around with us

00:00:29.000 --> 00:00:31.530
as we run through a quick demo
of the self-paced lab that's

00:00:31.530 --> 00:00:34.570
going to allow you to download,
test, and deploy an app.

00:00:39.930 --> 00:00:42.180
So Heather, what would you
focus on when considering

00:00:42.180 --> 00:00:43.290
a development platform?

00:00:43.290 --> 00:00:44.520
HEATHER CROSS: So
solid infrastructure

00:00:44.520 --> 00:00:46.020
is definitely top
of mind, and this

00:00:46.020 --> 00:00:47.430
is something that is
commonly brought up

00:00:47.430 --> 00:00:48.745
when I meet with developers.

00:00:48.745 --> 00:00:49.620
JENNY BROWN: Totally.

00:00:49.620 --> 00:00:52.410
App Engine's an integral part
of the Google Cloud Platform.

00:00:52.410 --> 00:00:55.650
It's going to provide everything
needed to build for the cloud.

00:00:55.650 --> 00:00:58.590
App Engine takes care of
deploying, running, and scaling

00:00:58.590 --> 00:01:00.570
your app, and this is
going to allow developers

00:01:00.570 --> 00:01:03.280
to spend more of their
time on building, testing,

00:01:03.280 --> 00:01:05.550
and deploying apps
for web, mobile,

00:01:05.550 --> 00:01:06.744
and even back end solutions.

00:01:06.744 --> 00:01:08.160
HEATHER CROSS: All
developers hope

00:01:08.160 --> 00:01:10.770
that their apps will go
big, so scalability is

00:01:10.770 --> 00:01:12.600
an important consideration too.

00:01:12.600 --> 00:01:14.400
With App Engine,
apps automatically

00:01:14.400 --> 00:01:17.400
scale at a moment's notice to
handle large or small amounts

00:01:17.400 --> 00:01:18.360
of traffic.

00:01:18.360 --> 00:01:21.139
Keep in mind that you only pay
for the capacity that you use.

00:01:21.139 --> 00:01:22.180
JENNY BROWN: That's true.

00:01:22.180 --> 00:01:24.660
A developer's data is
also very important,

00:01:24.660 --> 00:01:27.270
and App Engine provides a
range of choices for how data

00:01:27.270 --> 00:01:28.887
can be stored and retrieved.

00:01:28.887 --> 00:01:30.720
HEATHER CROSS: Oh, and
there's language too.

00:01:30.720 --> 00:01:32.710
Many developers have
a preferred language,

00:01:32.710 --> 00:01:35.940
so it's great that App Engine
supports a range of languages

00:01:35.940 --> 00:01:37.180
out of the box.

00:01:37.180 --> 00:01:39.390
This allows them to be
more productive immediately

00:01:39.390 --> 00:01:40.936
in a familiar environment.

00:01:45.746 --> 00:01:47.870
JENNY BROWN: So I'm pretty
confident that we've now

00:01:47.870 --> 00:01:49.730
answered the question,
why App Engine?

00:01:49.730 --> 00:01:51.230
HEATHER CROSS: Yeah,
I think so too.

00:01:51.230 --> 00:01:52.896
There are features,
of course, including

00:01:52.896 --> 00:01:56.420
being able to bring any library
and framework into App Engine,

00:01:56.420 --> 00:01:59.180
run multiple app versions
and micro-services,

00:01:59.180 --> 00:02:02.480
split traffic between versions,
access diagnostic for app

00:02:02.480 --> 00:02:04.887
monitoring and debugging,
and of course, app security.

00:02:04.887 --> 00:02:06.470
JENNY BROWN: Check
out the description

00:02:06.470 --> 00:02:08.313
below for some awesome
documentation that

00:02:08.313 --> 00:02:10.729
will get you well on your way
in your programming language

00:02:10.729 --> 00:02:11.229
of choice.

00:02:16.395 --> 00:02:17.770
HEATHER CROSS:
Now the fun part--

00:02:17.770 --> 00:02:18.820
Qwiklabs.

00:02:18.820 --> 00:02:20.300
To cater for
different languages,

00:02:20.300 --> 00:02:24.280
we have provided versions for
Python, Java, PHP, and Go.

00:02:24.280 --> 00:02:26.614
You can check out the links
below to start the Qwiklabs.

00:02:26.614 --> 00:02:28.113
JENNY BROWN: Each
of these labs will

00:02:28.113 --> 00:02:30.310
cover the activities of
downloading, testing,

00:02:30.310 --> 00:02:32.440
and deploying an app, and
each is going to take you

00:02:32.440 --> 00:02:34.824
about 30 minutes to complete.

00:02:34.824 --> 00:02:36.490
HEATHER CROSS: At
this point in the lab,

00:02:36.490 --> 00:02:39.610
we've cloned a simple Python
"hello world" application,

00:02:39.610 --> 00:02:41.830
and tested it using the
Google Cloud Development

00:02:41.830 --> 00:02:46.550
Server, which is included in the
pre-installed App Engine SDK.

00:02:46.550 --> 00:02:49.660
Next, you're going to deploy
the app to App Engine.

00:02:49.660 --> 00:02:52.300
You'll be prompted for a region.

00:02:52.300 --> 00:02:55.360
You'll be prompted to
confirm the deployment.

00:02:55.360 --> 00:02:59.865
You can then view your
deployed application.

00:02:59.865 --> 00:03:00.936
Well, there you have it.

00:03:00.936 --> 00:03:02.310
We hope you enjoyed
this episode,

00:03:02.310 --> 00:03:04.650
and would love to hear more
about how you use or would

00:03:04.650 --> 00:03:05.567
use Google App Engine.

00:03:05.567 --> 00:03:06.941
JENNY BROWN: Don't
forget to keep

00:03:06.941 --> 00:03:09.390
visiting our on air
webinar series, Qwiklabs,

00:03:09.390 --> 00:03:10.380
and our blogs.

00:03:10.380 --> 00:03:11.779
All those links are right below.

00:03:11.779 --> 00:03:14.070
HEATHER CROSS: You can also
learn more about App Engine

00:03:14.070 --> 00:03:16.180
through our on demand
courses on Coursera.

00:03:16.180 --> 00:03:17.170
Thanks for watching.

00:03:17.170 --> 00:03:19.907
Happy coding, and see you soon.

